\description{
For each replicate, simulate from the ground-truth specification, fit the model the
specification implies and test each focal fixed effect. The fitter follows the response family:
\code{lmerTest::lmer()} with Satterthwaite p-values for the continuous families, and \code{lme4::glmer()}
with a Wald z for accuracy and count responses (see Details). Reports power together with the
Type S and Type M errors of Gelman and Carlin (2014). Requires the \code{lme4} and \code{lmerTest}
packages.
}
\details{
\code{power_mixed()} is not a wrapper around an existing power package: it runs pilotr's own
simulation loop over the portable design specification. It covers territory pioneered by
\code{simr} (Green and MacLeod, 2016) and \code{mixedpower} (Kumle, Vo and Draschkow, 2021), to
which it is indebted. pilotr differs in being driven by the portable cross-language
specification, in reporting the Type S and Type M design-analysis errors alongside
power, and in parallelising its replicates through the \code{workers} argument.

The analysis model comes from the specification rather than from this function. Before 0.3 the
formula was written into the source as a maximal crossed structure, so a design declaring
uncorrelated slopes, or no slopes at all, was nonetheless analysed as though it had them, and a
design with more than one factor was refused. The formula now comes from \code{\link[=model_formula]{model_formula()}} and
the data from \code{\link[=model_data]{model_data()}}, so the analysis matches the process that generated the data. Both
can still be given directly, which is what to do when a deliberately different analysis model is
the point, as when checking how a misspecified model behaves.

The fitter follows the response family, so that the estimates are on the scale of the
specification's coefficients. A \code{gaussian}, \code{lognormal}, \code{shifted_lognormal} or \code{exgaussian}
response is fitted by \code{lmerTest::lmer()}, the two lognormal families on the log scale to which
\code{\link[=model_data]{model_data()}} takes them, and each effect is tested with Satterthwaite's degrees of freedom. A
\code{bernoulli} or \code{poisson} response is fitted by \code{lme4::glmer()} with the binomial or Poisson
family, on the logit or log scale, and each effect is tested with a Wald z. A model with no
random terms, such as that of a between-subjects design with one row per subject, is fitted by
\code{stats::lm()} or \code{stats::glm()} in the same way. The result's \code{fitter} names the fitter and the
test. A Wald z treats the estimate over its standard error as normal and so ignores the
uncertainty in the variance components. Bolker et al. (2009) recommend it for the fixed effects
of a GLMM without overdispersion, which these models are, but it rejects too often when the
clusters are few. In binary GLMMs with fewer than 30 clusters, a test with as many degrees of
freedom as observations, close to a z test, rejected more often than its nominal level (Li and
Redden, 2015). The power of a \code{bernoulli} or \code{poisson} design with few subjects or items is
therefore somewhat overstated.

pilotr has no frequentist model for an \code{ordinal} or \code{beta} response yet. Such a response is
fitted by the linear model on its own scale, while the specification's coefficients are on the
logit scale. The function therefore warns, and withholds the mean estimate and Type M (\code{NA}),
since both would compare the two scales. Type S compares signs alone, and the link keeps the
sign of a main effect unless an interaction reverses that effect between the levels of another
factor. It does not keep the sign of an interaction. A bounded response compresses differences
near the ends of its range, so an interaction that is positive on the logit scale can be
negative on the response scale. Type S is therefore withheld for an interaction as well. Power
is the rate at which the linear model's test rejects. \code{\link[=generate_design_analysis]{generate_design_analysis()}} writes a
Bayesian design analysis on the link scale, with the model \code{\link[=brms_bridge]{brms_bridge()}} derives.

A \code{formula} given here is fitted as written, by \code{lmerTest::lmer()}, or by \code{stats::lm()} when
it has no random terms, whatever the family, since only its author knows the scale of its
response. With such a formula, or a \code{prep} that changes the response, and \code{focal = NULL}, the
true values still come from the specification and are on its scale, which need not be the
model's. A named numeric \code{focal} gives the true values on the model's scale.

An analysis plan may instead preregister a simplification rule: fit the maximal model, and when
the fit is singular or fails to converge, drop the correlations, then the slopes (Barr et al.,
2013; Matuschek et al., 2017). \code{formula} given as a ladder of models simulates such a rule. Each
replicate fits the rungs in turn and keeps the first fit that \code{fallback} accepts, or the last
rung's fit when it accepts none. \code{c("maximal", "zcp", "intercepts")} names structures that
\code{\link[=model_formula]{model_formula()}} builds, which are fitted as the default model is, with the fitter the family
chooses. A list of formulas gives rungs that are fitted as written. Power, Type S and Type M are
then those of the rule, \code{rung_shares} gives the share of the replicates analysed at each rung,
and the fit counts describe the fit each replicate kept. A rule that can end on random
intercepts alone ignores, whenever it gets there, how much the effect varies over subjects and
items, and a test that ignores that variation rejects a true null hypothesis too often (Barr et
al., 2013). Run the null condition of \code{\link[=design_conditions]{design_conditions()}} through the same ladder to see how
often, as the power vignette does. A single formula is unaffected by \code{fallback}, and its result
is the one it always was.

Every reported rate carries its Monte Carlo standard error and a Wilson interval, because a
proportion over a finite number of replicates is an estimate rather than a fact. At the default
100 replicates a power near 0.5 has a standard error of 0.05. Type S and Type M average over
the significant replicates alone, which may be few. Each carries its own Monte Carlo error.
Type S has a Wilson interval, since its standard error would be zero whenever no sign is wrong.
Type M has its standard error, the standard deviation of the exaggeration ratios over the square
root of their number.

A directional prediction calls for a one-sided test, which \code{alternative} selects for every
focal effect. \code{"greater"} tests whether the coefficient is positive and \code{"less"} whether it is
negative. The one-sided p-value is the upper or lower tail of the statistic behind the
two-sided one. That is Satterthwaite's t, the t of \code{stats::lm()} with its residual degrees of
freedom, or a Wald z referred to the normal distribution. The one-sided p-value is half the
two-sided one when the estimate lies in the tested direction. A one-sided test at \code{alpha}
therefore rejects in exactly the replicates in which the two-sided test at \code{2 * alpha} rejects
with the estimate in that direction. Type S and Type M are taken over the replicates
significant in the tested direction. When that is the direction of the true effect, Type S is
0 by construction, and Type M is the exaggeration among the replicates that would support the
prediction. \code{fitter} names the direction, as in
\code{"lmerTest::lmer, Satterthwaite t, one-sided (greater)"}.

Results from earlier versions were two-sided. A one-sided power at \code{alpha}, in the direction of
the true effect, can be read off a two-sided result at \code{2 * alpha}: it is
\code{power * (1 - type_s)}. Type M is recovered only when that result's Type S is 0, since the
wrong-signed replicates cannot otherwise be told apart from the others.

A factor with more than two levels enters the model as several contrast columns, and its
interaction with another factor as several product columns. The hypothesis a study
preregisters about such an effect is then often the joint test of those coefficients, and its
power differs from that of any one of them. A named list as \code{focal}, such as
\code{list(interaction = c("A1_B", "A2_B"))} for the interaction of a three-level factor with a
two-level one, plans that test. Each set is tested against the hypothesis that all its
coefficients are zero. A linear mixed model has Satterthwaite's F from \code{lmerTest::contest()}
(Kuznetsova et al., 2017), a \code{glmer()} or \code{glm()} fit has the Wald chi-square of the set's
estimates and their covariance matrix, and an \code{lm()} fit has the F test that compares the model
with and without the set. A set of one coefficient therefore gives the p-value of the
single-coefficient test, with F equal to t squared or a chi-square equal to z squared. Under a
ladder of models, each set is tested on the fit the simplification rule kept. \code{fitter}
names the joint test, as in \code{"lmerTest::lmer, Satterthwaite F"}. A joint test has no direction,
so \code{alternative} must stay \code{"two.sided"}. Nor has it a single estimate, so the true value, the
mean estimate, Type S and Type M are \code{NA}. A likelihood-ratio test is not offered, since it would
refit every replicate by maximum likelihood and is anti-conservative for fixed effects in small
samples (Pinheiro and Bates, 2000).
}
\examples{
\donttest{
if (requireNamespace("lme4", quietly = TRUE) &&
    requireNamespace("lmerTest", quietly = TRUE)) {
  spec <- build_spec(list(name = "p", seed = 1, design_kind = "within",
    include_items = TRUE, n_subject = 12, n_item = 12, factor_name = "cond",
    lev1 = "a", lev2 = "b", intercept = 6, effect = 0.05,
    subj_int_sd = 0.12, subj_slope_sd = 0.04, subj_corr = 0.2,
    item_int_sd = 0.08, item_slope_sd = 0.02, item_corr = -0.1,
    family = "shifted_lognormal", resp_name = "", sigma = 0.3, shift = 200))
  # n_sims is small so the example runs quickly. Use 200 or more for real planning.
  power_mixed(spec, n_sims = 10)
}
}
}
